                            {
                                "example_id": "63d9254a-48cb-42df-9aed-ca28fa955211",
                                "sense_id": "6a4a4f92-fdcf-393a-9402-c916a5d9aa4a",
                                "text": "By the time of the Domesday Book in 1086, the Weald (the area between the North and South Downs, through Sussex, Hampshire, Kent and Surrey) was still 70 per cent woodland or wooded pastureland, but other counties like Devon, Yorkshire and Cornwall were less than 10 per cent wooded."
